WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.097 --> 00:00:03.612
- [Narrator] In China's Huanglong Valley,

00:00:03.612 --> 00:00:07.403
you can find 2.2 miles of
naturally formed hot springs.

00:00:07.403 --> 00:00:10.736
Just follow the yellow, calcified trail.

00:00:16.195 --> 00:00:18.952
These colorful ponds have
formed over 1,000 years

00:00:18.952 --> 00:00:21.828
by geologic change, causing
the limestone to mold

00:00:21.828 --> 00:00:23.764
and the crystal clear waters to overflow

00:00:23.764 --> 00:00:26.347
into the series of pools below.

00:00:27.219 --> 00:00:29.082
On either side of this natural wonder

00:00:29.082 --> 00:00:31.832
are rugged mountains and forests.

00:00:33.106 --> 00:00:35.719
From a distance, the cream
yellow rock of the hot springs

00:00:35.719 --> 00:00:37.575
leaves through the forest, standing out

00:00:37.575 --> 00:00:39.075
as a steamy oasis.

00:00:39.992 --> 00:00:41.874
The color and shape result in the locals

00:00:41.874 --> 00:00:45.214
calling the pools the Yellow Dragon.

00:00:45.214 --> 00:00:48.361
Huanglong Valley is also home
to the Huanglong Monastery,

00:00:48.361 --> 00:00:49.550
and one of its remaining temples

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:52.784
can be found at the top
of the pools' terraces.

00:00:52.784 --> 00:00:54.654
For many years, the area
was under the threat

00:00:54.654 --> 00:00:57.736
of industrialization,
but through local efforts

00:00:57.736 --> 00:01:00.529
and being a UNESCO site, the Yellow Dragon

00:01:00.529 --> 00:01:02.223
will be seen in its natural beauty

00:01:02.223 --> 00:01:04.223
for generations to come.

